PART 3 — ESTABLISH A DISTINCTIVE CRESTIQTRADES BRAND SYSTEM

3.1 Primary color direction

The primary visual direction must be built around:

* Deep black and near-black.
* Clean white.
* Carefully selected blues.
* Restrained neutral grays.

Purple must not be a dominant brand color.

Remove the generic purple-and-blue combination that commonly appears in automatically generated AI interfaces. Do not replace it with an equally generic arrangement of bright cyan, electric blue, and glowing gradients.

Blue must be used with discipline.

It should help users identify important actions, active states, selected controls, key information, and brand elements. It should not be sprayed across every card, icon, border, heading, and decorative object.

Establish a controlled palette with documented design tokens. Use the following as initial direction rather than an excuse to apply the same colors everywhere:

* Primary dark: approximately #0B0D10.
* Secondary dark: approximately #11151B.
* White: approximately #FFFFFF.
* Soft white: approximately #F5F7FA.
* Primary blue: approximately #246BFD.
* Supporting blue: approximately #4A8DFF.
* Muted text on dark surfaces: approximately #A7B0BD.
* Muted text on light surfaces: approximately #5F6875.
* Subtle borders: neutral gray with sufficient contrast.
* Positive financial states: a restrained, readable green.
* Negative financial states: a restrained, readable red.

These are starting points, not a requirement to use every token in every section.

Test contrast, legibility, and appearance across actual page compositions. Adjust the precise shades when necessary to achieve a more refined result.

Do not use bright blue for every button. Do not use green as a decorative brand color everywhere merely because it is associated with trading.

3.2 Control the color distribution

Use dark and light surfaces deliberately.

A dark hero section can transition into a white or soft-neutral information section. A dark AI feature can be followed by a bright explanatory section. A detailed trading interface can use a darker workspace surrounded by carefully organized neutral surfaces.

This variation gives the website rhythm.

Avoid making every section a dark rectangle stacked on another dark rectangle.

Avoid making every section white either.

The design should have a planned visual progression from the top of the page to the footer.

The transitions must feel natural and deliberate, with consistent container widths, alignment, typography, and spacing.

Do not use gradients to compensate for weak composition.

