PART 4 — TYPOGRAPHY MUST FEEL CONFIDENT, PROFESSIONAL AND READABLE

Typography is one of the main reasons an interface looks professionally designed or cheaply generated.

The current thin-looking typography must be corrected.

4.1 Font selection

Inspect the existing fonts and identify where they are being used.

Choose a professional, highly legible sans-serif typeface appropriate for a serious fintech product.

Potential directions include Inter, Manrope, or another well-designed contemporary sans-serif. Choose a suitable typeface based on actual visual performance rather than automatically combining several fashionable fonts.

Do not load numerous font families without a clear reason.

Prefer a small, coherent type system.

If using an externally hosted font, ensure that it loads reliably. Where practical, use appropriately licensed font assets and sensible fallback stacks.

4.2 Font-weight requirements

Do not use very thin font weights for important interface elements.

Establish a clear hierarchy, for example:

* Hero headings: approximately 700–800 weight.
* Main page headings: approximately 700.
* Section headings: approximately 650–750.
* Financial figures: approximately 650–750.
* Card titles: approximately 600–700.
* Navigation: approximately 500–600.
* Body text: approximately 400–450.
* Supporting text: approximately 400–450.
* Button labels: approximately 600.

Adjust these weights to suit the selected font.

The important requirement is that text must not look weak, faded, or unnecessarily delicate.

Do not make all text bold. Strong typography depends on contrast between weights, sizes, line lengths, and spacing.

4.3 Headings

Use large, confident headings for major sections.

Give important headings appropriate line breaks and sufficient space around them.

Avoid the generic pattern of a small colored eyebrow label, a large two-line heading, a short paragraph, and three identical cards repeated across every page.

That pattern can work occasionally, but it must not become the entire application’s design language.

Each major section needs an appropriate composition.

4.4 Financial figures and numbers

Financial values must be easy to scan.

Use readable, sufficiently weighted numerals.

Where supported by the selected font, use tabular numerals for aligned monetary values and tables.

Maintain consistent formatting for currencies, percentages, quantities, and timestamps.

Do not display decorative decimals, random balances, fabricated returns, or arbitrary percentages merely to make a screen look active.

If an account has no real data, present a clear empty state rather than inventing figures.

4.5 Responsive typography

Define a deliberate scale for desktop, tablet, and mobile.

Prevent oversized headings from overflowing narrow screens.

Prevent important headings from shrinking until they become difficult to read.

Check line wrapping, paragraph widths, button labels, tables, navigation, and numeric values on small screens.

Typography must be tested in the real interface, not just selected from a design-token list.

